{"type":"text","timestamp":1793704825759,"sessionID":"ses_e5edc9a8dffeY2qk12Q5MgI31P","part":{"id":"prt_1a1238d59001WYRTCFBoElQVA0_text-0","sessionID":"ses_e5edc9a8dffeY2qk12Q5MgI31P","messageID":"msg_1a1238d59001WYRTCFBoElQVA0","type":"text","text":"Done. Here's the summary:\n\n## Change made\n\n**File:** `schedule.py` (line 7)\n\n```python\n# Before\nwhile start < end:\n# After\nwhile start <= end:\n```\n\n## Why\n\nThe README contract states that `business_days` \"counts **both endpoint dates** when weekdays and not holidays.\" The original code used `while start < end`, which excluded the end date — so a single-day range like `business_days(date(2026,11,2), date(2026,11,2))` returned `0` instead of `1`. Changing `<` to `<=` makes the loop inclusive of both endpoints, matching the documented behavior.\n\n## Test result\n\n```\n....\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 4 tests in 0.000s\n\nOK\n```\n\nAll 4 tests pass:\n- `test_same_weekday` — single-day range now returns 1 ✓\n- `test_week` — full week Mon–Fri returns 5 ✓\n- `test_holiday` — holiday exclusion still works (4 of 5 days) ✓\n- `test_reversed` — reversed range still raises `ValueError` ✓","time":{"start":1793704824156,"end":1793704825759}}}
